 "note": "Two rings, one inside the other, like a ladder bent round (half-turn symmetry): the outer rail curves round each home on Curve 60s, the inner rail is a ring of 2 m half bridges round the hill. Both rails break at the top and bottom, where a rotor sits between them: r1 closes the outer ring, r2 the inner one. Retract relays on the inner rail hold the half-bridge rungs to the homes and the long spokes to the hill.",
 "geometricSymmetry": true,
 "labVariant": {
  "junctions": true
 },
 "experimental": true,
 "ruleWaivers": {
  "S03": "half-turn symmetry: the front door (camera side) is the same for both seats, so the sim walk from each home differs by a few seconds - the same as the live point-symmetric pool maps N-11 / N-14 / N-20 / N-22 / N-24 / N-28 (first measured 2026-10-28, junction fairness walk); pending Daniele",
  "S04": "half-turn symmetry: the front door (camera side) is the same for both seats, so the sim walk from each home differs by a few seconds - the same as the live point-symmetric pool maps N-11 / N-14 / N-20 / N-22 / N-24 / N-28 (first measured 2026-10-28, junction fairness walk); pending Daniele",
  "N13": "rotor -> switch (rulebook pass 2026-10-28): the two states join decks of different tiers / spacing, which N13 forbids for a rigid rotor; the connections per state are the original's",
  "N03": "the switch hub IS the crossing by design (every state keeps 2+ decks, so the game's _islands counts its decks as fixed and never strands a side); N03 reads 'relays only add shortcuts' - Daniele's call on hub crossings"
 },
